[1m[95m[NARRATION][0m 
  Kael's fingers find the familiar weight of the brass compass in his pack. He 
  draws it out — the casing worn smooth by years of handling — and holds it 
  across the table. Mara's eyes track the movement. She doesn't reach for it. 
  Instead, she tilts her head. A single lamp on the wall sputters. Rain drums 
  against the shutters. "That compass," she says slowly, "has been in your hand 
  since you walked in. You fidget with it when you're thinking. You touched it 
  when I mentioned Old Bram's name." She doesn't move to take it. "I asked you 
  to hand it over for safekeeping, didn't I? And you're offering it now." She 
  folds her arms. "But here's the thing, Kael — I don't want it because I asked 
  for it. I want you to tell me why you're giving it to me." She lets the 
  silence stretch. "What changed between 'no' and 'here'?" 

