[1m[95m[NARRATION][0m 
  Kael's fingers find the coins in his pouch — two gold, warm from his pocket. 
  He sets them on the scarred table between them. They clink once, settle. 
  Mara's eyes drop to the coins. Then back up to you. A beat of silence. Rain 
  against the shutters. Then she reaches out, scoops them up without looking, 
  and drops them into her coat pocket. No hesitation. "Fair price." She almost 
  smiles. "Most people try to pay me in promises." She leans back, one hand 
  resting on the iron reliquary. The bronze pin catches the lamplight — a dull, 
  patient gleam. "Two gold buys you my full attention, Kael. And right now, my 
  full attention is on three things." She counts them off on her scarred hand. 
  "One — the key. Bram had it. Someone took it. Wherever it is, that's where 
  this starts." "Two — the gate. I've been watching it for three days. No one 
  approaches. No one leaves. But *something* came through the night Bram died. 
  I heard boots on the ferry slip. Fast. Heading toward the city." "Three — the 
  reliquary." She taps the iron box. "Someone wanted me to pull this pin. That 
  means someone *wants* the gate open. Which means someone on the other side 
  wants in." She fixes you with a look. "So. Where do we start?" 
