  Mara's hand disappears into her coat — not the pocket where the gold went, 
  but an inner lining, stitched close to her ribs. Her fingers find something, 
  close around it, and emerge. She sets it on the table. Not a key of iron or 
  brass. This one is obsidian — black as still water, carved with a single 
  groove running its length. It catches the lamplight like a splinter of night 
  sky. "I found it three days ago," she says quietly. "Wedged in the ferry 
  slip's mooring post. Half-hidden in the wood. Bram must have dropped it — or 
  *placed* it there — before he died." She doesn't touch it again. Just watches 
  it sit between them, black against the scarred oak. "I didn't show it to 
  anyone. Didn't trust anyone." Her eyes lift to yours. "Until now." The 
  reliquary hums with its dormant charge. The rain keeps its rhythm on the 
  shutters. And the obsidian key lies on the table — waiting for your hand. 
  Mara's voice drops. "That gate hasn't opened in three days. But this key 
  might be the only thing that can." 
