[1m[95m[NARRATION][0m 
  Your fingers close around the obsidian key. It's colder than it should be — 
  not the chill of morning air, but something deeper, like touching stone 
  that's never seen the sun. The single groove along its length feels 
  deliberate, a guide for something yet to come. It slides into your pack. The 
  weight settles against the compass. Across the table, Mara exhales — a breath 
  she might have been holding since she first pulled the key from her coat 
  lining. "There. It's yours now." She almost sounds relieved. "I've been 
  carrying that thing for three days, and every night I dreamed someone was 
  reaching for it." She rubs her scarred palm absently. The reliquary sits 
  between you. Still. Patient. Its bronze pin glints. Mara's eyes track to the 
  shuttered window. Rain streaks the glass. Beyond it, somewhere in the grey 
  morning, the Ash Gate waits. "So. We have a key. We have a gate. We have a 
  reliquary that someone wanted me to open." She ticks them off. "And we have a 
  dead ferryman whose last act was wedging that key into a post." She looks 
  back at you. "Question is — do we go to the gate first, or do we find out who 
  sent the reliquary before we walk into whatever's waiting on the other side?" 
