[1m[95m[NARRATION][0m 
  The word hangs in the air like a held breath. *Now.* For a heartbeat — 
  nothing. Mara's eyes snap to you, confusion flickering across her face. Then 
  the reliquary answers. A click from inside the iron box. Precise. Deliberate. 
  The bronze pin shudders once, then *drops* — sliding home into a recess that 
  wasn't there a moment ago. Mara's hand shoots out — too late. She's already 
  moving back, chair scraping the floorboards. The iron box *cracks*. Not an 
  explosion. Not fire. A clean, surgical fracture — iron splitting along seams 
  you couldn't see, the bronze pin shearing in two. A puff of grey smoke rises. 
  No heat. No blast. Just the sound of metal giving way to something that was 
  *waiting* inside it. The reliquary falls apart in two clean halves. They 
  clatter onto the scarred oak table and lie still. Smoke curls upward, thin 
  and grey, and dissipates against the copper lamps. Silence. Then the rain 
  again. The shutters. The old tavern breathing. Mara stares at the wreckage. 
  Her hand is still half-raised, frozen mid-reach. Slowly, she lowers it. 
  "You—" She stops. Starts again. "*That* was the trigger?" She looks from the 
  sundered iron to you, something new in her eyes. Not fear. Not anger. 
  *Understanding.* "You knew. The whole time. You knew what word would finish 
  it." She exhales. Runs a hand through her hair. "Damn, Kael." A rough laugh, 
  barely there. "I was *holding* that thing. I was going to *pull the pin.*" 
  She looks at the wrecked reliquary, then back at you. "Whoever sent it — they 
  wanted me to say that word. Not pull the pin. *Speak.*" Her jaw tightens. 
  "They knew I'd read the note aloud. They knew I'd say 'now' when I was 
  ready." The two halves of the iron box lie open on the table. Inside the 
  hollow of one half — nestled in a felt-lined compartment, untouched by the 
  mechanism that destroyed its container — is a single strip of paper. Mara 
  sees it at the same time you do. Her eyes meet yours. "Well?" she says 
  quietly. "Are we reading it, or burning it?" 

