[1m[95m[NARRATION][0m 
  You turn from the door. Face the empty tavern. The copper lamps. The scarred 
  tables. The rain against glass. "Bram." The name falls into the quiet. No 
  echo — the tavern swallows it, damp and heavy. Silence answers. The way 
  silence always answers the dead. You don't wait for more. You *remember*. 
  Three days ago. The ferry slip. Muddy water lapping at the pilings. Old 
  Bram's body — still warm when you found him, sprawled on the wet boards, his 
  ferryman's coat twisted, one hand open and empty. The other clenched so tight 
  you had to pry the fingers apart. Nothing in them. But you remember what he 
  said the last time you saw him alive. The evening before. Standing right 
  here, at this very bar, a half-empty glass in his hand. His voice — gravel 
  and weariness — cutting through the tavern murmur. *"That gate ain't just 
  locked, boy. It's* watching. *Something on the other side's been listening. 
  Waiting. You go near it after dark, you feel it breathing back at you through 
  the keyhole."* He'd drained his glass. Set it down hard. *"I've ferried folk 
  across that slip for twenty years. Never seen what's on the other side. But 
  I've seen what comes* through. *And none of it was ever looking to shake 
  hands."* Then he'd left. Walked out into the night. And by morning, he was 
  dead on the slip, his key gone, the gate sealed. The front door rattles 
  again. Harder. *"Last warning! Open the door!"* Mara's hand is still on the 
  rear latch. The alley breathes cold air. The obsidian key weighs against your 
  hip. Bram's warning hangs in the air between you and the empty tavern. 

