[1m[95m[NARRATION][0m 
  You step past Mara into the alley. The latch clicks behind you. Rain catches 
  your face — a thin, persistent drizzle that's been falling since before dawn. 
  The Copper Finch's back door disappears into the grey morning as you move. 
  The streets of the dockside district are empty. Curtains drawn. Chimneys 
  cold. The rain has scrubbed the cobblestones clean, leaving them dark and 
  slick. Your boots find the path without thought — left at the chandler's, 
  past the empty fish stalls, through the arch where the cobbles give way to 
  packed earth. The buildings thin. The wharves fall behind. And then you see 
  it. The Ash Gate rises at the dead end of a narrow lane, wedged between two 
  tenement walls that have been left to crumble. It's old — older than the 
  city, older than the stone around it. Black iron, streaked with rust that 
  looks almost like dried blood, set into a frame of cracked basalt. The arch 
  above it is split by a single fracture that runs from the keystone to the top 
  curve, as if something on the other side once pushed hard enough to *almost* 
  break through. No handle. No hinges visible. Just a dark sheet of iron, 
  seamless and still, set into the stone like a scar. The keyhole is at waist 
  height. Iron-rimmed. Dark. The obsidian key waits in your pack. Rain runs 
  down the gate's surface in thin rivulets, tracing paths through the rust. Up 
  close, you can see scratches around the keyhole — recent ones, from something 
  metal and impatient. Someone's been here. Trying. Failing. The air around the 
  gate is still. Colder than the morning around it. And if you press your ear 
  close — if you *listen* — there's nothing. Silence so complete it feels like 
  the gate is holding its breath. 

